# Chapter Four — Before Coffee

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.2. Written against the working crime engine, which remains unratified. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 6 — Coffee

= Present: Henry, Ruth, Lydia, Edwin, Marian, Robert, Nell, Paul.

= Immediate pressure: Paul intends to retrieve the carton downstairs before he speaks to Nell. Ruth needs to know why a sentence from June has appeared in her own life, but does not yet want Henry to make a question of it.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - NIGHT

The waiter clears the dessert plates. Edwin speaks to him at the kitchen door, shakes his hand, and lets him go.

EDWIN
Coffee will be at the bar.

LYDIA
Are we being dismissed from the table?

EDWIN
You have completed dinner.

ROBERT
I haven't completed the tart.

EDWIN
Bring it.

Robert does.

Edwin opens one panel of the glass roof. The night air enters slowly. Chairs move back. Nobody decides to stand at the same time, and soon the round table is no longer the shape of the room.

Edwin pours coffee at the service bar. Robert accepts a cup, tastes it, and waits for Edwin to look at him.

ROBERT
It's strong.

EDWIN
Yes.

ROBERT
I like it.

EDWIN
Thank you for making both remarks.

Nell carries her cup to the open roof panel. Marian joins her. Lydia remains at the table, gathering nothing into a very small pile: her napkin ring, the place where her glass stood, one grain of salt pressed beneath her finger.

Ruth pours a cup and puts it in Henry's hand.

HENRY
No sugar.

RUTH
You didn't use any at the office.

HENRY
You didn't drink the coffee.

RUTH
I watched you ruin yours.

She makes her own with a little milk. Paul comes from the other side of the bar and adds another drop before she can stop him.

RUTH
That was enough.

PAUL
It wasn't.

She tastes it. He was right. She says nothing.

Paul stays beside her. He says something too low for Henry to hear the start of it.

PAUL
—if I say it badly.

RUTH
Then you said it.

PAUL
You always—

RUTH
Not this one.

Paul nods as if she has told him the time. Henry hears the end of it and lets it go.

Paul pours a cup and leaves it beside Edwin's hand before taking one for himself. Edwin is still explaining the coffee to Robert. He finds the cup a moment later, looks across at Paul, and drinks it.

Henry looks toward Lydia, then back to Ruth.

HENRY
June's sentence.

RUTH
I've seen something like it.

HENRY
Where?

RUTH
At work.

HENRY
Does Paul know why?

Ruth looks across the room. Paul is listening to Edwin explain why the Winter Room stopped serving orange ice.

RUTH
He looked as if he did.

HENRY
Do you want me to ask him?

RUTH
No.

Henry drinks his coffee.

RUTH
That's all?

HENRY
You said no.

RUTH
You make it sound reasonable.

HENRY
It was one syllable.

RUTH
You have no idea how much can be done with one syllable.

Paul returns with his cup.

PAUL
Edwin says they stopped making orange ice in 1968.

RUTH
I didn't ask.

PAUL
Nell did.

RUTH
Then tell Nell.

Paul looks toward the roof panel. Nell is near enough to hear him.

PAUL
They stopped making orange ice in 1968.

NELL
I heard.

PAUL
Good.

Nell smiles into the open air. Paul takes a drink.

Across the room, Edwin has left Lydia's place cards on the service bar beside a stack of saucers. Marian finds them and fans them in one hand.

MARIAN
Where did you intend to put Henry?

LYDIA
Beside me. One side or the other.

MARIAN
Every time?

LYDIA
I changed my mind about the other side.

MARIAN
Who was there?

LYDIA
You. Then Robert. Then no one.

MARIAN
Where did you put me finally?

EDWIN
It no longer matters.

Marian turns her card over. A faint number in green ink has been crossed out twice.

MARIAN
It mattered to someone.

Lydia holds out her hand. Marian gives her the card.

LYDIA
I wanted you close enough to speak to me.

MARIAN
You could have called.

LYDIA
I did. You came here.

Marian looks at the card again in Lydia's hand.

MARIAN
I suppose I did.

Lydia tears the card once and puts the two halves beneath her coffee saucer.

Robert arrives with his tart and looks between them.

ROBERT
Did I miss something?

MARIAN
For thirty years.

ROBERT
Which thing?

Marian takes his arm. He gives her the last bite of tart.

Paul sets down his cup and takes the brass key from beside his plate.

PAUL
The lift goes down to the motor court?

EDWIN
Turn the key at the cosmetics floor and hold it. The lift will take you one level lower. Mr. Bell will meet you.

PAUL
I can find him.

EDWIN
He knows where he is.

Paul puts on his jacket. The cream envelope rises from his inside pocket. Ruth pushes it down with two fingers as he passes her.

PAUL
Come with me.

RUTH
Bring the carton up.

PAUL
I need you to hear something first.

RUTH
Nell hears it first.

PAUL
I don't know if I can.

Nell has come back to the table. She hears him.

NELL
I can't do that part for you.

Paul looks at her.

NELL
Say it badly. Just say it yourself.

Paul nods.

PAUL
I need to speak to Ruth downstairs.

NELL
Then go.

Ruth sets down her coffee.

RUTH
I am not explaining anything for you.

PAUL
I know.

RUTH
Then do it.

She takes her jacket from the back of the chair and her handbag from under it.

She puts it on and buttons it.

Paul goes to the service lift and unlocks it. Ruth remains beside Henry for one moment.

RUTH
Watch my coffee.

HENRY
It hasn't moved yet.

RUTH
Good.

The lift door opens. Paul and Ruth enter. The wire-glass door closes after them.

Henry watches the floor indicator descend. When it reaches three, Marian asks him whether husbands are easier to find before or after dinner.

He turns to answer her.

## Scene 7 — The returned key

= Present: Henry, Lydia, Edwin, Marian, Robert, Nell; then Ruth.

= Immediate pressure: The gathering continues in the absence of two people. No one yet has a reason to call that absence dangerous.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - NIGHT

MARIAN
After dinner.

HENRY
Usually.

MARIAN
Why?

HENRY
They get hungry.

ROBERT
That sounds right.

MARIAN
No one is looking for you.

ROBERT
Not tonight.

Nell sits in Paul's chair. She does not touch the envelope because it is no longer there. Lydia sees where she has chosen to sit and brings her the coffee pot.

NELL
No, thank you.

Lydia pours half a cup anyway.

NELL
That is exactly what I meant.

Lydia sets the pot down.

LYDIA
I heard you.

NELL
You disagreed with a cup.

Lydia pushes the cup away from Nell and takes it for herself.

LYDIA
There.

Nell watches her drink it.

NELL
Better.

At the service bar, Edwin measures grounds for a second pot. It has begun to drip by the time Robert brings him two empty cups.

Edwin takes the cups from him without looking away from the pot.

The service lift begins to rise. Henry looks at the indicator. Nell does not.

The lift opens. Ruth steps out alone. She has her handbag under her arm instead of on it.

There is a pale smear along the outside of one shoe. Henry takes it for dust from the carton.

She takes the brass key from her jacket pocket and places it beside Edwin's hand.

EDWIN
Where is Mr. Ellery?

RUTH
Downstairs.

Nell turns.

NELL
With the carton?

RUTH
Yes.

NELL
Is he coming back?

Ruth takes the coffee she left beside Henry. She touches it to her mouth and sets it down.

RUTH
He said not to wait.

NELL
He said the opposite to me.

RUTH
He changed his mind.

LYDIA
Was Mr. Bell with him?

RUTH
He was downstairs.

Henry looks at Ruth.

HENRY
What happened?

RUTH
He wanted me to tell Nell for him. I refused.

HENRY
And then?

RUTH
He said he needed to think.

NELL
You know?

RUTH
Not all of it.

NELL
Did he tell you what was in the carton?

RUTH
No.

Nell gets up from Paul's chair.

MARIAN
Give him a little while.

NELL
He has already had a long while.

MARIAN
I know.

Nell looks toward the lift.

She returns to her own chair.

Edwin picks up the brass key and puts it back in his jacket. Robert asks whether there is any coffee left. Edwin pours him some.

Ruth sits beside Henry. Her coffee remains between them, cooling.

Robert tries the second pot and says it is stronger than the first. Edwin tells him it is the same coffee. Around them, conversation starts again in smaller pieces.
